    {
      "note": "The discontinuous for … sake frame stays whole on the expressed preposition under rule 78 and rule 15, and heads on sake. ἕνεκεν governs one case only and forms no verbal compound, so rule 95 classifies it improper — it is that rule's own worked example — and rule 71 settles an improper preposition's complex frame on the frame's lexical noun: the noun is what renders the adverb's own sense and the supplied for belongs to the English idiom rather than answering to the governed case, which is rule 42's reasoning. Rule 78's eleven for … sake units all carry a proper preposition, διά or ὑπέρ, so they are no precedent here, exactly as rule 95 says of the by reason of units that head on by. A head on for is the competing reading and would follow rule 78's bare sentence rather than its evidence.",
